Wayne National Forest - Ironton Unit	Natural Area			Active	United States federal government	USDA Forest Service, Wayne National Forest, Ironton Ranger District			The Ironton Unit is the southernmost of three units comprising Wayne National Forest, Ohio's only national forest. The unit covers 107,090 acres across Lawrence, Gallia, Scioto, and Jackson counties, with over two-thirds of acreage in Lawrence County. Primary developed recreation areas (Lake Vesuvius Recreation Area, Hanging Rock OHV Trail System) are located in Lawrence County. The Scioto County portion consists of dispersed non-contiguous federal parcels used for dispersed hiking, hunting, and wildlife habitat.	Ironton Ranger District; ranger station at Pedro, Ohio; Scioto County parcels are dispersed across the county	107090	Gallia;Jackson;Lawrence;Scioto			38.609158	-82.648099	86CVJ952+MQ	Hiking;Horseback riding;OHV trails;Hunting;Fishing;Dispersed camping;Wildlife viewing	Multi-county entity; Scioto County is one of four counties in the Ironton Unit. The unit is a non-contiguous 'checkerboard' pattern of federal ownership mixed with private land. No specific named recreation sites confirmed within Scioto County parcels from web research; Lake Vesuvius (143 ac lake, campgrounds, beach, 25+ mi hiking, 46-mi horse trails) and Hanging Rock OHV (26 mi) are in Lawrence County; Symmes Creek trail system is in Gallia County. WNF website blocked during discovery — Scioto County trail inventory incomplete, flagged for verification.		https://www.fs.usda.gov/r09/wayne;https://www.fs.usda.gov/r09/wayne/recreation/ironton-ranger-district-ironton-area-0		2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
