Shawnee State Forest	Natural Area			Active	State of Ohio	Ohio Department of Natural Resources, Division of Forestry	Shawnee Bridle Club (trail maintenance partnership)		Ohio's largest state forest at 63,747 acres, spanning Scioto and Adams counties in the Appalachian foothills near the Ohio River. Nicknamed 'The Little Smokies' for its rugged terrain and fog-filled hollows. Contains approximately 8,000 acres of designated wilderness — the largest contiguous wilderness in Ohio. Managed for timber, recreation, and wildlife habitat. Contains the Shawnee Backpack Trail (40+ mi, includes Buckeye Trail and North Country Trail segments), 60+ miles of bridle trails, mountain bike trails (recently added), and numerous day hiking trails.	13291 US-52, West Portsmouth, Ohio 45663; Scioto and Adams counties; western Appalachian foothills	63747	Adams;Scioto		Nile	38.703268	-83.088892	86CRPW36+8C	Backpacking;Hiking;Horseback riding;Mountain biking;Hunting;Fishing;Dispersed camping;Horsemen's campground (20 sites near Bear Lake);Wildlife viewing;Wilderness area (~8,000 ac);Bridle trails (60+ mi)	Largest state forest in Ohio. Entity encompasses Shawnee State Forest management unit; Shawnee State Park (SC-S-0005 pending) is a separate 1,095-acre ODNR Parks unit adjacent to and surrounded by the Forest, administered by a different ODNR division. Child historic sites: CCC Memorial to Company 1545 (Ohio Historic Site #50, SC-S-0009 pending) and Copperhead Fire Tower (Ohio Historic Site #6, SC-S-0010 pending). Wilderness: approximately 8,000-acre designated wilderness area is the largest contiguous wilderness in Ohio. Horsemen's camp: 20-site facility near Bear Lake on State Forest Service Road 6. Mountain bike trails confirmed to exist at Shawnee State Forest (Trailforks page for 'Shawnee State Forest, West Portsmouth Mountain Biking Trails') but specific named trails not confirmed from web search — flagged PENDING VERIFICATION. GPS is headquarters reference point (38.7032684, -83.0888916) at 13291 US-52, West Portsmouth, OH 45663 — forest spans two counties; no meaningful centroid exists. GPS confirmed via Google Maps. Primary county: Scioto (county of record for forest headquarters and majority of use). Scioto County Shawnee Bridge to Bridge Route: 36-mile shared-roadway bicycle route from Otway Covered Bridge to Mackletree Bridge passing through portions of the forest — assess for Trail entity at Tier 8 or note.		https://forestry.ohiodnr.gov/shawnee;https://www.backpackohio.com/trails/shawnee-state-forest-backpack-trail/;https://www.sohiotrails.org/trails/shawnee-backpacking-trail/;https://www.trailforks.com/region/shawnee-state-forest-36317/		2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
Shawnee State Park	Park			Active	State of Ohio	Ohio Department of Natural Resources, Division of Parks and Watercraft			A 1,095-acre state park nestled within Shawnee State Forest, offering a full range of park amenities. Features Shawnee State Lodge (50 rooms), two lakes (Roosevelt Lake and Turkey Creek Lake, 68 acres combined), a marina on the Ohio River (72 docks), 10 hiking trails, and a campground opened in 2023. The Black Bear Disc Golf Course is on-site. Mini golf available. A golf course operated until 2019 then closed.	4404 OH-125, West Portsmouth, Ohio 45663; surrounded by Shawnee State Forest	1095	Scioto		Nile	38.739795	-83.203587	86CRPQQW+WH	Hiking (10 trails);Lodging (Shawnee Lodge, 50 rooms);Marina (Ohio River, 72 docks);Camping (campground opened 2023);Fishing;Boating;Disc golf (Black Bear course);Mini golf;Two lakes (Roosevelt Lake and Turkey Creek Lake, 68 ac total)	GPS confirmed via Google Maps (38.7397953, -83.2035869); address: 4404 OH-125, West Portsmouth, OH 45663. Acreage discrepancy: baseline records 1,163 ac; AllTrails and park literature show 1,095 ac; both figures preserved. Shawnee State Park is distinct from Shawnee State Forest (SC-S-0004) and is administered by ODNR Division of Parks and Watercraft (not Division of Forestry). The Shawnee Backpack Trail (SC-T-0001) traverses both units. Day hiking trails within the park boundary (Lampblack, Park Loop, Knighton, Lookout, Campground Loop) are separate Trail entities (SC-T-0002 through SC-T-0006). Golf course closed 2019; area repurposed for Black Bear Disc Golf Course. Campground opened 2023 — new facility not in pre-2023 documentation.		https://parks.ohiodnr.gov/shawnee;https://dam.assets.ohio.gov/image/upload/ohiodnr.gov/documents/parks/parkmaps/shawneeparkmap.pdf		2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
Gladys Riley Golden-star State Nature Preserve	Nature Preserve			Active	Arc of Appalachia (fee ownership of larger 230-acre parcel)	Ohio Department of Natural Resources, Division of Natural Areas and Preserves (state nature preserve designation); Arc of Appalachia (on-the-ground management)	Arc of Appalachia	State-private partnership: ODNR designates and oversees SNP; Arc of Appalachia owns land and manages preserve	A State Nature Preserve protecting golden star lily (Erythronium rostellatum), an Ohio Threatened species, and one of the finest examples of this rare wildflower community in the state. The ODNR state nature preserve designation covers 186 acres within the larger 230-acre Arc of Appalachia preserve. Features a floodplain trail and an oak-hickory hillside trail. Permit required for access.	Tick Ridge-Koenig Hill Rd, Otway, Ohio 45657; Scioto County, Ohio	186	Scioto		Brush Creek	38.850620	-83.201882	86CRVQ2X+66	Hiking (two trails: floodplain trail and oak-hickory hillside trail);Rare plant observation (golden star lily, Ohio Threatened);Wildflower study;Permit required	GPS confirmed via Google Maps (38.8506197, -83.2018817); access via Tick Ridge-Koenig Hill Rd, Otway, OH 45657. Dual-record entity: this record represents the ODNR state nature preserve designation (186 acres). The Arc of Appalachia owns the full 230-acre parcel, recorded separately as 'Gladys Riley Golden Star Lily Preserve' at Tier 8. The 44-acre difference reflects a 2015 Arc of Appalachia acquisition of 118 adjacent acres; the ODNR SNP boundary was set at 186 ac prior to that expansion and has not been revised to encompass the full Arc parcel. These are not duplicate records: ODNR manages the conservation designation; Arc manages the land. Acreage discrepancy documented per pre-discovery analysis. Two trails confirmed: floodplain trail (lower) and oak-hickory hillside trail (upper). Permit required — contact ODNR DNAP.		https://naturepreserves.ohiodnr.gov/gladysriley;https://arcofappalachia.org/gladys-riley-golden-star-lily-preserve/		2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
Raven Rock State Nature Preserve	Nature Preserve			Active	State of Ohio	Ohio Department of Natural Resources, Division of Natural Areas and Preserves			A 95-acre permit-only state nature preserve protecting a dramatic Mississippian sandstone landscape featuring three natural arches (largest span approximately 10 feet) and an overlook approximately 500 feet above the valley floor — identified as the tallest known natural overlook in Ohio. A 1.25-mile one-way hiking trail provides access. Home to the potentially threatened blackjack oak (Quercus marilandica) and the state endangered small-flowered scorpion weed (Phacelia dubia). Dedicated as a state nature preserve in 1996.	Scioto County, Ohio	95	Scioto		Washington	38.718660	-83.054733	86CRPW9W+F4	Hiking (1 hiking trail / 1.25-mile one-way);Three natural sandstone arches;500-ft overlook (tallest known in Ohio);Rare geology;Rare plant species;Permit required	GPS confirmed via Google Maps (38.7186598, -83.0547332). Acres corrected to 95 from ODNR sources (permit access page lists 95; ODNR Natural Arches page cites 1996 dedication at 95 ac; prior secondary source had 98). Dedicated 1996 (not 1993 as noted in some secondary sources). Mississippian sandstone bedrock. Three natural arches; largest ~10-ft span. 500-ft overlook — tallest known in Ohio. Rare species confirmed from ODNR page: blackjack oak (Quercus marilandica, potentially threatened) and small-flowered scorpion weed (Phacelia dubia, state endangered). Natural features: Unique Geology, Wildflowers, Woods. 1 named hiking trail per ODNR page. Permit required — contact ODNR DNAP. ODNR URL updated to new format (ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/...). ODNR also maintains a page for 'Raven Rock Arch' — assess whether separate child Site entity or feature page for same preserve before creating child record.		https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/raven-rock-state-nature-preserve		2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
Scioto Brush Creek State Nature Preserve	Nature Preserve			Active	State of Ohio	Ohio Department of Natural Resources, Division of Natural Areas and Preserves			A 30-acre permit-only state nature preserve on Scioto Brush Creek protecting exceptional aquatic biodiversity and riparian habitat. The creek supports over 70 fish species including longnose gar and rosyside dace. Features mature trees, spring wildflowers, and rare species. ODNR has added new facilities to this preserve (date and details not confirmed). The broader Scioto Brush Creek was designated Ohio's 17th State Scenic River in November 2025, covering 25.1 river miles from the Adams-Scioto county line to the confluence with the Scioto River.	Tatman-Coe Rd, McDermott, Ohio 45652; within the Scioto Brush Creek watershed, Scioto County	30	Scioto		Union	38.841431	-83.094960	86CRRWR4+H2	Nature study;Aquatic biodiversity;Rare species observation;Spring wildflowers;Mature trees;New facilities (details not confirmed);Permit required	GPS confirmed via Google Maps (38.8414307, -83.0949596); access via Tatman-Coe Rd, McDermott, OH 45652. ODNR preserve page blocked during discovery; acreage (30 ac) and permit-only status confirmed from secondary sources. New facilities reference per ODNR news post — details unavailable due to page block; may include parking, signage, or trail infrastructure. Scioto Brush Creek State Nature Preserve (this record) is distinct from Scioto Brush Creek State Scenic River (SC-S-0012 pending): the SNP is a 30-acre land parcel; the Scenic River is a 25.1-mile water corridor designation, both managed by ODNR DNAP.		https://naturepreserves.ohiodnr.gov/sciotobrushcreek;https://naturepreserves.ohiodnr.gov/natural-areas-preserves-home/post/new-facilities-at-scioto-brush-creek-state-nature-preserve		2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
CCC Memorial to Company 1545	Historic Site				State of Ohio	Ohio Department of Natural Resources, Division of Forestry (within Shawnee State Forest)			Ohio Historic Site #50. A memorial to Civilian Conservation Corps Company 1545, which performed conservation and forest development work in Shawnee State Forest during the New Deal era of the 1930s. Located within the bounds of Shawnee State Forest.	Within Shawnee State Forest, Scioto County, Ohio		Scioto						Historic interpretation;Memorial;CCC history	ODNR Historic Site #50. Parent entity: Shawnee State Forest (SC-S-0004 pending). Child site of the forest; no independent public access point — accessed via Shawnee State Forest road system. CCC Company 1545 performed Depression-era conservation work at Shawnee; the memorial commemorates their contribution. Exact location within the forest not confirmed from web research — GPS flagged for acquisition. No dedicated ODNR web page found during discovery.				2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
Copperhead Fire Tower	Historic Site			Active	State of Ohio	Ohio Department of Natural Resources, Division of Forestry (within Shawnee State Forest)			Ohio Historic Site #6. The first fire lookout tower built in Ohio, constructed in 1924. A 60-foot steel tower on Copperhead Hill at an elevation exceeding 1,200 feet, affording a 360-degree panoramic view of Shawnee State Forest and the Ohio River valley. The tower has been restored and is open for climbing at visitors' own risk. Listed on the National Historic Lookout Register.	Copperhead Hill, State Forest Service Road 6, West Portsmouth, Ohio 45663; within Shawnee State Forest		Scioto		Union	38.772609	-83.171484	86CRQRFH+2C	Historic fire tower (60-ft steel, 1924);360-degree panoramic view;Tower climbing at own risk;Restored;National Historic Lookout Register	GPS confirmed via Google Maps (38.7726088, -83.1714841); West Portsmouth, OH 45663 (within Shawnee State Forest on State Forest Service Road 6). ODNR Historic Site #6. Parent entity: Shawnee State Forest (SC-S-0004 pending). First fire tower in Ohio (1924); 60-foot steel construction. Copperhead Hill elevation >1,200 ft. National Historic Lookout Register confirmed. Tower restored and re-opened; visitors may climb at own risk. The named AllTrails trail 'Copperhead Firetower and Bear Lake' (SC-T-0009) provides access to this site from a trailhead within the forest.		https://www.alltrails.com/trail/us/ohio/copperhead-firetower-and-bear-lake		2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
Shawnee State University — Deal Arboretum and Campus Trails	Curated Biological Site			Active	State of Ohio (Shawnee State University, state institution)	Shawnee State University			The Shawnee State University campus hosts the Deal Arboretum, an ArbNet Level 2 accredited arboretum named after Professor Robert Deal. The arboretum contains over 400 trees representing approximately 100 species, integrated throughout the campus grounds. Four named self-guided tree trails provide structured walking routes: Appalachian Tree Trail, Around the World Tree Trail, Relics of the Past Tree Trail, and Medicinal Tree Trail. The campus and trails are publicly open.	940 Second Street, Portsmouth, Ohio 45662 (Shawnee State University campus)		Scioto	Portsmouth		38.739800	-82.997700	86CVP2Q2+WW	Self-guided tree trails (4 named trails);400+ trees;~100 species;ArbNet Level 2 accreditation;Public access;Campus arboretum	ArbNet Level 2 accreditation (more rigorous than basic Level 1). Named after Professor Robert Deal. Four named tree trails are Trail entities (SC-T-0010 through SC-T-0013 pending). Publicly open — not restricted to SSU students or staff. GPS is an approximate campus centroid derived from known street address; arboretum has no single GPS centroid. Clark Planetarium (also on SSU campus) excluded per pre-discovery analysis: university building with no confirmed conservation or natural areas function. Campus trails URL: https://www.shawnee.edu/campus-life/trees/campus-trails (blocked during discovery — SSU trail names confirmed from search snippets).		https://www.shawnee.edu/campus-life/trees/campus-trails		2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
Scioto Brush Creek State Scenic River	Water Site				State designation (Ohio Scenic Rivers Program; water corridor)	Ohio Department of Natural Resources, Division of Natural Areas and Preserves			Ohio's 17th State Scenic River, designated by Governor DeWine in November 2025. The designation covers 25.1 miles of Scioto Brush Creek from the Adams-Scioto county line downstream to the creek's confluence with the Scioto River. Protects exceptional aquatic biodiversity (70+ fish species including longnose gar and rosyside dace), native hardwood riparian habitat, and 171 documented plant and animal species. ODNR and partners manage approximately 23,000 acres of public land within the Scioto Brush Creek watershed, including state forests, nature preserves, and natural areas.	25.1 river miles from Adams-Scioto county line to confluence with Scioto River, Scioto County, Ohio		Adams;Scioto						Paddling;Fishing;Aquatic biodiversity (70+ fish species);Riparian habitat;Scenic river corridor;Watershed conservation	Designated November 2025 — post-knowledge-cutoff; confirmed via web search during Scioto County discovery. Scioto Brush Creek State Scenic River is distinct from Scioto Brush Creek State Nature Preserve (SC-S-0008): the SNP is a 30-acre land parcel; the Scenic River is a 25.1-mile water corridor designation. Both managed by ODNR DNAP under different programs. Multi-county: corridor spans Adams and Scioto counties. Primary county: Scioto (downstream/confluence end). GPS: no meaningful centroid for a 25.1-mile river corridor — blank. Not in baseline seeds; discovered during Tier 2 state research. Ohio Scenic Rivers designation does not confer public land ownership; it is a regulatory/conservation designation.		https://naturepreserves.ohiodnr.gov/sciotobrushcreek;https://spectrumnews1.com/oh/columbus/news/2025/11/20/scioto-brush-creek-named-ohio-s-17th-state-scenic-river		2026-03-28T18:06:52Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
Brush Creek State Forest	Natural Area			Active	State of Ohio	Ohio Department of Natural Resources, Division of Forestry			Located in the unglaciated hill country of south central Ohio, Brush Creek State Forest was established in 1928 with the acquisition of 285 acres of land. This and other early land acquisitions were originally part of Shawnee State Forest. As acreage and distances grew, the need to form a separate state forest unit became apparent in order to properly manage these scattered land holdings. In the early 1950s, a new state forest was formed and drew its name from a major stream in the area known as Scioto Brush Creek. Formerly, the forest headquarters and service center were established along State Route 73 about one mile west of the village of Rarden in western Scioto County. Today, Brush Creek State Forest comprises well over 13,000 acres of productive hardwood forest land. The vast majority of these acres is made up of steep hillsides, deep hollows, and narrow ridge tops.	Unglaciated hill country of south central Ohio, spanning Adams, Pike, and Scioto counties; former HQ on State Route 73 approximately 1 mile west of Rarden, Scioto County; currently administered from Pike State Forest, 334 Lapperell Road, Latham, OH 45646	13000	Adams;Pike;Scioto		Rarden	38.928729	-83.265958	86CRWPHM+FJ	Hunting;Hiking;Horseback riding;Mushroom and berry picking;Historical stone quarries;Stone Quarry trail;Coffee Hollow & Crabtree Cemetery bridle trail;Wildlife viewing;Forest roads	TIER 2 MISS — discovered during Tier 4 county research via Scioto County government recreation page. Not in baseline seeds. Multi-county state forest spanning Adams, Pike, and Scioto counties. Former HQ was on SR-73 near Rarden in western Scioto County; administrative office has moved to Pike State Forest (334 Lapperell Road, Latham, OH 45646, Pike County). Phone (740) 858-6685 shared with Shawnee State Forest administration. GPS not confirmed — no authoritative coordinate for the Scioto County portion published on ODNR page; blank pending GPS acquisition. Currently administered from Pike State Forest.		https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/brush-creek-state-forest;https://cdn.outerspatial.com/uploads/media_file/uploaded_file/2529/brushcreek.pdf		2026-03-31T12:11:18Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
Earl Thomas Conley Riverside Park	Park			Active	Scioto County	Scioto County (County Commissioners / Scioto County Parks Department)			Earl Thomas Conley Riverside Park is located at 15888 U.S. 52 West of Portsmouth and is named for local native and Country Music legend Earl Thomas Conley. The park features 81.2 acres of natural landscape along the Scioto and Ohio rivers, offering picnic shelters, a walking and biking trail, fit path, frisbee golf, splash pad, pump track and more. The Allan W. Eckert Trail offers visitors views of the banks and bottom lands at the confluence of the Scioto and the Ohio Rivers.	15888 U.S. 52, West Portsmouth, OH 45663	81.2	Scioto		Washington	38.718075	-83.047183	86CRPX93+64	Picnic shelters;Walking and biking trail (Allan W. Eckert Trail);Fit path;Frisbee golf;Splash pad (Doug Coleman Memorial Splash Pad);Pump track (Red Bull Pump Track — largest in Ohio);Tennis courts;Beach volleyball courts;Basketball courts;Baseball field;Parking;Restrooms	County-managed park confirmed via Scioto County Commissioners (Commissioner Scottie Powell referenced construction projects; ETC Park Manager Brian Howard manages the park). Address 15888 US-52 is in unincorporated West Portsmouth area. Note: portsmouthohio.org (City of Portsmouth website) also references the splash pad at ETC Park — city may have some involvement or the website is a regional information site; county management confirmed from news sources and county recreation page listing. Doug Coleman Memorial Splash Pad is a named feature/child facility within the park — not staged as separate child Site (recreational facility, not natural area character). Red Bull Pump Track is also a named feature. Allan W. Eckert Trail is a named trail at the park — staged separately. Baseline seed: Earl Thomas Conley Park (listed in baseline as Portsmouth City Park — corrected to Scioto County per research).		https://www.sciotoheritagetrail.com/earl-thomas-conley-park.html;https://www.discoveringanew.com/blog-4/earl-thomas-conley-riverside-park-a-day-of-family-fun-in-the-sun		2026-03-31T12:11:18Z	2026-03-31T12:11:18Z
